  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Weathers threw gas, but Rice broke the deadlock when it mattered most.",
    "what_this_means": "This is a team that doesn't need to control a game to win it. Weathers delivered seven scoreless frames through nine. The kind of starter line that usually demands run support early and often. Instead, Toronto tied it at two, and for six innings nothing shifted. Rice's home run in the tenth ended that standoff, a swing that converted what could have been a slow loss into a gutty one. The bullpen held the door. Bednar and Hill combining for nearly three scoreless innings in a game that refused to break open until the end. They scored just four runs total. Four was enough because Weathers made it count, because Rice found one when the game was still tied, because the back end didn't blink. This is how narrow margins compound into wins.",
    "what_to_watch": "Baltimore starts Shane Baz tonight at 6:35 PM, a right-hander sitting at a 4.00 ERA. Rod\u00f3n has won three of his last four. The Yankees get a pitcher trending up against an offense that struggles against velocity.",
